{
  "dom_id": "step-q87488",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "The graph of $y=4f(2x)-3,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Translate up by $3$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Translate down by $3$ units\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch by a scale factor of $0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?",
  "worked_solution": "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=4f(2x)-3,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4).$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}=0.5$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2).$ Stretch $y=f(2x)$ by a scale factor of $4$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=4f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3).$ Translate $y=4f(2x)$ down by $3$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=4f(2x)-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}3).$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
